meta {
  name: bulk update entries (tags + status + storage location)
  type: http
  seq: 17
}

post {
  url: {{collection_url}}/entries/bulk-update
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

body:json {
  {
    "entry_ids": ["{{entry_id}}", "{{entry_id_2}}"],
    "add_tag_ids": ["{{tag_id}}"],
    "status": "shelved",
    "storage_location": "closet shelf B"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.updated_count: eq 2
}

docs {
  Run create-entry, create-second-entry, and create-tag first
  (entry_id, entry_id_2, tag_id). One transaction across both entries:
  attaches tag_id to both, sets status shelved and storage_location on
  both. Expect 200 {"updated_count":2}. Re-running answers the same
  count (idempotent: the tag is already attached, the fields already
  hold these values).

  Foreign or unknown ids in entry_ids are silently not counted, same
  posture as tag attachment. Guard failures (entry_ids outside 1-200,
  add_tag_ids/remove_tag_ids over 50, an unknown status,
  storage_location over 200 characters, or no action present at all)
  answer 400 invalid_body before the store runs. The per-entry 50-tag
  cap answers 400 tag_cap_exceeded and rolls back the WHOLE call - no
  partial apply, even for entries well under the cap in the same
  batch.

  A status change into or out of backlog manages backlog_rank like the
  single-entry update: entering appends at the end, already-backlog
  entries keep their position, leaving clears it.
}
